course: everyday
lesson: 272
title: Bikes, scooters and car parks
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A helmet protects only if it fits: snug, level on the head, just above the eyebrows, with the strap buckled — a loose helmet slides off when you need it.
explain: Drivers must be able to see you, so at night a bike has a white light at the front, a red light at the back and reflectors; and you never ride up the inside of a lorry or truck at a junction, where the driver cannot see you and may turn across you.
explain: Wheels that are hard to stop — scooters on steep hills, skateboards and roller skates — belong in parks and skate parks, not busy streets.
explain: In car parks and parking garages cars come round corners in the dark, so children stay close to an adult and never ride on a shopping trolley.
explain: A girl in Melbourne rides on a shared path; she rings her bell well before the people walking and passes them slowly.
fact: A helmet fits snug and level, just above the eyebrows, with the strap buckled; a loose helmet does not protect.
fact: Rules about riding on the pavement differ by country and town; where children may, they watch out for people walking.
fact: At night a bike needs a white light at the front, a red light at the back and reflectors, so drivers can see it.
fact: A bike is locked by its frame and a wheel to a fixed bike rack, because unlocked bikes are stolen.
fact: You never ride up the inside of a lorry or truck at a junction, because the driver cannot see you and it may turn.
fact: With a puncture you walk the bike home or call a parent, and you can learn to fix it later.
fact: On a path shared with walkers you ring your bell and pass slowly, because people walking may not hear you.
fact: You walk a scooter down a steep hill, because you can lose control.
fact: Skateboards and roller skates are hard to stop quickly, so they are used in parks and skate parks, not on busy streets.
fact: A rolling shopping trolley or cart is stopped by an adult, and children never ride on trolleys, because they can tip over and hit cars.
fact: In a parking garage you stay close to the adult, because it is dark and cars come round corners.
exercise: Your helmet slides back off your forehead. What should you do? | Tighten it so it is snug, level, just above the eyebrows | Push it back on your head and ride anyway | Leave the strap open so it is comfortable | Wear it tilted back
exercise: Why does a loose helmet not protect you? | It can slide off or move when you fall | Loose helmets are made of weaker plastic | A tight helmet makes you ride much faster | Helmets only ever work properly in the rain
exercise: Can children ride bikes on the pavement? | It depends on local rules; if allowed, watch for walkers | Yes, everywhere in the world, as long as you are under twelve | No, never anywhere | Only if they ride very fast
exercise: What does a bike need for riding at night? | A white light in front, a red light behind and reflectors | A red light in front and a white light behind, plus reflectors | A bell and a basket | Only a bright coloured frame
exercise: Why does a bike need lights at night? | So drivers can see you | So you can read signs in the dark | To make the bike go faster | To scare animals away from the road
exercise: How do you lock your bike in town? | Lock the frame and a wheel to a fixed bike rack | Lock just the front wheel on its own | Lean it against a shop window unlocked | Lock it to a small sign that can be lifted out of the ground
exercise: A lorry is waiting at a junction and there is a gap on its inside. What do you do? | Stay behind it and never ride up its inside | Ride up the inside to get to the front before the lights change | Squeeze past while it waits | Knock on the door to tell the driver
exercise: Why is riding up the inside of a truck at a junction dangerous? | The driver cannot see you and it may turn across you | Trucks drive slower than bikes | The truck might honk and scare you off your bike into the kerb | The road is bumpy there
exercise: Your tyre gets a puncture far from home. What do you do? | Walk the bike home or call a parent | Keep riding on the flat tyre | Leave the bike on the path | Ask a stranger to take you in their car
exercise: You ride on a path shared with walkers. How do you pass them? | Ring the bell and pass slowly, leaving room | Ride past fast so it is over quickly | Ride close behind until they move | Shout at them to get out of the way
exercise: You come to a steep hill on your scooter. What do you do? | Get off and walk down it | Ride down as fast as you can | Ride down with your eyes closed | Sit on the scooter and slide down
exercise: Where should you use your skateboard or roller skates? | In a park or a skate park | On a busy shopping street | In the road next to the kerb | On the steps of a station
exercise: A shopping trolley starts rolling away across the car park. What do you do? | Let an adult stop it safely | Chase it between the cars | Jump on it to stop it | Push it harder so it goes straight
exercise: Why should children never ride on a shopping trolley? | It can tip over and hit cars | Trolleys are only for food | It makes the wheels squeak | It is too slow to be fun
exercise: You walk through a dark parking garage with your mum. What do you do? | Stay close to her the whole time | Run ahead to find the car | Play hide and seek between cars | Walk in the middle of the lane
test: A boy in Denver wears his helmet pushed far back with the strap undone. Which is true? | It may not protect him in a fall | It protects him just as well when loose | It is safer because he can see more | Straps are only needed for racing
test: A girl rides home at dusk in Dublin with no lights. What is the biggest danger? | Drivers may not see her | She may get lost in the dark | Her bike will go slower at night | Her tyres will go flat in the cold
test: A cyclist waits beside a bus that is about to turn left in London. Why is this dangerous? | The bus may turn across her, and the driver cannot see her | Buses never turn at junctions | She will breathe in the bus fumes | The bus driver will be cross with her and sound the horn very loudly
test: A boy locks only his front wheel to a post. What can a thief do? | Take the rest of the bike and leave the wheel | Nothing, because the bike is locked to the post | Only take the bell | Only take the lights
test: Why might walkers on a shared path not notice a bike coming? | Bikes are quiet and they may not hear it | They always look behind them every few steps | Bikes are too big to miss | Walkers can feel bikes coming
test: A girl in San Francisco wants to scooter down a very steep street. What is wise? | Get off and walk the scooter down | Ride down and brake at the bottom | Lean forward to go faster | Ride with one foot on the ground
test: Why are skateboards not good on busy streets? | They are hard to stop quickly | They are not allowed anywhere | They are too heavy to carry | They always break on concrete
test: A boy's tyre is flat. He has no tools. Which is the best choice? | Walk the bike home or phone a parent | Ride on it, because it is not far | Hide the bike in a bush | Get a lift home from a friendly stranger who stops to help
test: In a dark parking garage, why do children stay close to an adult? | Cars come round corners and drivers may not see them | The lights might go out | Children are not allowed in garages without a ticket | It is warmer near adults
test: helmet's loose n tilted back, its fine | No, it should be snug, level and buckled | Yes, a loose helmet still works | Tilt it more for air | Wear it unbuckled on short rides
test: lorry turning left at the lights, i can squeeze up the inside on my bike | No, never ride up the inside of a lorry | Yes, if you're quick before the lights change | Yes, bikes have right of way | Only if you ring your bell
test: big steep hill, gonna scoot down it super fast | Walk the scooter down, you can lose control | Lean back and go | Go faster so it's over quickly | Drag your feet hard to stop at the bottom of the hill
test: riding home at night, no lights on my bike, its ok i know the way | You need a white front light, red back light and reflectors | It's fine if you know the way | Just wear a dark coat | Ride in the middle of the road where drivers can see you better
explain: A boy in Melbourne rode his bike without his helmet just to the shop; he hit a kerb, fell head first and needed weeks in hospital to recover from a head injury.
explain: A girl in London rode up the inside of a lorry at a junction; the lorry turned left without seeing her and she was knocked off and badly hurt.
fact: A good helmet greatly lowers the chance of a serious head injury when you fall off a bike or scooter.
fact: Many falls happen on short everyday trips near home, so the helmet goes on for every ride, even just round the corner.
fact: Lorry and truck drivers have big blind spots beside their cab, where they cannot see a cyclist at all.
test: only going round the corner, dont need my helmet right | Wear it, most falls happen on short everyday trips | Right, short trips are always safe | Only on main roads | Only if it is raining and the road is slippery
test: can i squeeze past the truck on the inside at the lights | No, wait behind it, the driver cannot see you there | Yes, if you go fast | Yes, trucks always go straight on at lights | Yes, if you ring your bell
test: kid in the car park on a scooter, cars reversing | Get off and walk beside an adult, drivers may not see you | Ride faster to get out quickly | Ride behind the cars where it is quiet | Ride between the parked cars
test: did u hear?? a kid came off his scooter on the big hill and his helmet was just hanging off the handlebars, my mum says he cracked his head. lesson? | Wear the helmet buckled and walk down steep hills | Helmets don't help so don't bother | Scooters are only safe on hills | Just ride faster to keep your balance, wobbling is what makes you fall
test: ppl reckon a cyclist got "flattened by a lorry, totally squished" at the lights in town. what's the true bit to learn | Never ride up the inside of a lorry at a junction | Lorries can see every bike around them | Bikes should squeeze past lorries at lights | Cyclists are safest right beside the cab, where the driver can see them
test: the boys say the girl who fell off the shopping trolley "deserved it, so dumb". spread it? | No, don't pass on mean talk; and never ride on trolleys | Yes, it's funny and it's true | Yes, and make a meme of it | Yes, but only in the group chat where the teachers can't see it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct fitting of a cycling helmet? | It sits snug and level above the eyebrows, strap fastened | It rests loosely towards the back of the head | It is worn unfastened to permit ventilation | It is carried upon the handlebars until required
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a cyclist shall not proceed along the inside of a heavy goods vehicle at a junction. | The driver cannot see the cyclist and may turn across them | Heavy goods vehicles are prohibited from turning at junctions | The inside lane is reserved exclusively for buses | The cyclist would thereby delay the traffic signals
exam: Pursuant to the requirements for cycling after dark, select the equipment that a bicycle must carry. | A white front light, a red rear light and reflectors | A single red light mounted at the front | A bell of sufficient loudness in place of lights | Reflective tape upon the rider's helmet only, worn above the eyes
exam: State the course of action appropriate to a scooter rider confronted with a steep downward gradient. | The rider shall dismount and walk the scooter down | The rider shall descend at speed in order to remain stable | The rider shall drag one foot along the ground to slow down | The rider shall lean back and hold the handlebars loosely
